1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:06,154
An animal that has spent its entire life in permanent darkness, seven kilometres below the

2
00:00:06,154 --> 00:00:09,940
Pacific. A scientist opens its gut. Inside is a blue fibre.

3
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:15,880
Not on the seafloor beside it. Inside it. In the hindgut, where food goes.

4
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,684
The animals they examined are amphipods.

5
00:00:18,684 --> 00:00:22,380
Scavengers, about the size of your thumb, and the dominant scavenging animal in the deepest

6
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:25,020
places on Earth. Wherever something dies down there, they arrive.

7
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:30,806
In twenty nineteen, a team published what they found after examining ninety of them, pulled

8
00:00:30,806 --> 00:00:33,190
from six of the deepest trenches on the planet.

9
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:38,989
Over seventy two percent of the individuals examined contained at least one microparticle.

10
00:00:38,989 --> 00:00:40,690
Sixty five animals out of ninety.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:47,548
And in the deepest trench of all, the Mariana, the figure was not seventy two percent.

12
00:00:47,548 --> 00:00:48,730
It was one hundred.

13
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:54,224
Every single animal they brought up from the bottom of the world had something synthetic inside

14
00:00:54,224 --> 00:00:54,360
it.

15
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:59,539
This is the story of how we measured that, what else is down there with it, and the one

16
00:00:59,539 --> 00:01:01,680
question the measurements still cannot answer.

17
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:04,340
You're watching mindwired.

18
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,420
What they found inside the deepest animals on Earth.

19
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:12,978
To understand what was found, you have to understand where.

20
00:01:12,978 --> 00:01:15,880
And the where is stranger than most people picture.

21
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:21,023
Oceanographers give the deepest water its own name. The hadal zone.

22
00:01:21,023 --> 00:01:25,270
It begins at six thousand metres and runs to just under eleven thousand.

23
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:30,269
It is not a layer that covers the ocean. It is a set of holes in it.

24
00:01:30,269 --> 00:01:35,534
Trenches, mostly around the rim of the Pacific, narrow and steep and separated from each other

25
00:01:35,534 --> 00:01:38,110
by thousands of kilometres of shallower water.

26
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:43,884
At the bottom of the deepest one, the water above you weighs down at more than a thousand times

27
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:45,220
the pressure at the surface.

28
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,790
You cannot simply go there.

29
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:51,920
Almost everything we know about hadal animals comes from a machine that falls.

30
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:57,078
A baited lander is dropped over the side of a ship with weights attached.

31
00:01:57,078 --> 00:01:58,382
It free falls for hours.

32
00:01:58,382 --> 00:02:02,350
It sits on the bottom in the dark with a camera and a trap, and it waits.

33
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:07,650
Then it releases the weights and floats back up, and whatever came to the bait comes with it.

34
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:13,860
What comes to the bait, almost always, is amphipods. They arrive in their thousands.

35
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:18,940
In the hadal zone they are the animals that clean up everything that dies above them.

36
00:02:20,170 --> 00:02:22,350
Which makes them the perfect animal to test.

37
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:25,570
Whatever is falling into the trench ends up passing through them.

38
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:30,856
Before we get to the contamination, one animal deserves its own chapter.

39
00:02:30,856 --> 00:02:35,060
Because it got there first, and what it gave up to stay is extraordinary.

40
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:40,264
Before we get to what is in them, it is worth knowing what lives down there at all.

41
00:02:40,264 --> 00:02:42,290
Because the answer is stranger than empty.

42
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:46,327
This is the hadal snailfish. Pseudoliparis swirei.

43
00:02:46,327 --> 00:02:51,680
It lives between six and eight thousand metres, and it is the deepest vertebrate in the ocean.

44
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:58,970
It has no scales. It is barely pigmented. It looks, honestly, like something unfinished.

45
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:04,556
And in twenty twenty three, a team sequenced its genome and found something nobody expected

46
00:03:04,556 --> 00:03:06,400
about how long it has been down there.

47
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:12,069
The hadal snailfish, they wrote, may have entered and fully adapted to such extreme

48
00:03:12,069 --> 00:03:14,660
environments only in the last few million years.

49
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:19,310
A few million years. In evolutionary terms that is recent.

50
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:24,220
And having got there, it spread into separate Pacific trenches within a million years more.

51
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:30,712
Getting there cost it. The genome shows it lost its pigmentation. It lost visual genes.

52
00:03:30,712 --> 00:03:33,520
It lost genes for calcifying its own skeleton.

53
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:40,426
And it gained things. A compound called T M A O that stops proteins collapsing under pressure.

54
00:03:40,426 --> 00:03:45,850
And a duplicated gene for ferritin, which raised its tolerance to the reactive oxygen that

55
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:48,080
would otherwise tear its cells apart.

56
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:54,680
For decades that was almost everything we knew. We had specimens. We did not have behaviour.

57
00:03:55,860 --> 00:04:00,981
Then, this year, a team put a baited lander at nearly seven thousand metres, filmed what came,

58
00:04:00,981 --> 00:04:05,340
and tracked eight hundred and sixty eight separate sightings of individual fish.

59
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:10,926
It moves slowly. About seven tenths of its own body length every second.

60
00:04:10,926 --> 00:04:14,720
A gentle, unhurried animal in the most hostile place there is.

61
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:17,357
And it finds food by smell.

62
00:04:17,357 --> 00:04:22,483
The same study estimated it first picks up the scent of bait from about three hundred and fifty

63
00:04:22,483 --> 00:04:23,130
metres away.

64
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,917
That is the world we are talking about.

65
00:04:26,917 --> 00:04:32,950
Sparse, slow, patient, and until very recently, untouched by us in any way we could measure.

66
00:04:34,150 --> 00:04:35,438
Now the measurement.

67
00:04:35,438 --> 00:04:40,910
Ninety animals, six trenches, and a method simple enough to describe in one sentence.

68
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:46,986
The microplastics study is the one with the hardest numbers, so it is worth being precise about

69
00:04:46,986 --> 00:04:47,610
what it did.

70
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:54,847
Six trenches around the Pacific Rim. Japan. Izu Bonin. Mariana. Kermadec. New Hebrides.

71
00:04:54,847 --> 00:05:01,120
And Peru Chile. Depths from seven thousand metres to ten thousand eight hundred and ninety.

72
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:05,069
From those six trenches, ninety amphipods.

73
00:05:05,069 --> 00:05:10,090
The researchers opened the hindgut of each one and looked for anything that was not food.

74
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:15,940
Over seventy two percent of them had at least one. Sixty five animals out of ninety.

75
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:22,466
I want to be careful with that number, because ninety animals is a small sample and the honest

76
00:05:22,466 --> 00:05:26,334
thing is to say so out loud. This is not a census of the deep ocean.

77
00:05:26,334 --> 00:05:29,860
It is ninety animals, from six places, examined one at a time.

78
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:34,500
But the pattern inside those ninety is very hard to look away from.

79
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:39,702
The frequency varied by site, between fifty percent and one hundred.

80
00:05:39,702 --> 00:05:43,089
The lowest was the New Hebrides Trench, at fifty percent.

81
00:05:43,089 --> 00:05:46,180
The highest was the Mariana, at one hundred percent.

82
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:52,120
The deepest trench on Earth had the highest contamination rate of all six.

83
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:54,700
Not the one nearest a city. The deepest one.

84
00:05:55,940 --> 00:06:00,218
Most of what they found was not fragments of hard plastic. It was fibre.

85
00:06:00,218 --> 00:06:05,030
Fibres appeared in eighty four percent of the animals. Fragments in only sixteen.

86
00:06:06,230 --> 00:06:11,070
And one item dominated. Blue fibres, in sixty six percent of all the animals.

87
00:06:11,070 --> 00:06:16,916
With, and I am quoting the paper, all amphipods sampled from the Mariana Trench containing at

88
00:06:16,916 --> 00:06:18,110
least one of these.

89
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:23,820
A blue thread. In every animal, from the deepest place there is.

90
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:32,144
They ran a subsample through infrared spectroscopy to find out what the fibres actually were.

91
00:06:32,144 --> 00:06:38,740
Nylon. Polyethylene. Polyamide. Polyvinyl alcohol. P V C, often with inorganic filler.

92
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:44,340
Rayon and lyocell, which are semi synthetic. And ramie, which is a plant.

93
00:06:45,570 --> 00:06:47,606
That list is not a list of ocean things.

94
00:06:47,606 --> 00:06:51,830
It is a list of the things our clothes and our ropes and our packaging are made of.

95
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:56,180
And then the paper makes the comparison that I think is the whole story.

96
00:06:57,370 --> 00:07:02,351
The animals in the hadal zone are ingesting plastic at, in the paper's words, a similar

97
00:07:02,351 --> 00:07:06,530
frequency, seventy two percent, to crustaceans in coastal water habitats.

98
00:07:08,190 --> 00:07:09,049
Read that again.

99
00:07:09,049 --> 00:07:14,039
The deepest, most isolated animals on the planet are as likely to have plastic in them as the

100
00:07:14,039 --> 00:07:15,810
ones living just off our beaches.

101
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:18,971
The plastics are the visible half.

102
00:07:18,971 --> 00:07:24,360
The other half is chemical, it is older, and most of it was restricted before anyone reading

103
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:25,180
this was born.

104
00:07:26,370 --> 00:07:31,769
Two years before the plastics paper, the same lead researcher published something quieter and,

105
00:07:31,769 --> 00:07:32,630
I think, worse.

106
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:37,860
Not what the animals had swallowed. What had accumulated in their tissue.

107
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:40,701
Persistent organic pollutants.

108
00:07:40,701 --> 00:07:45,659
Industrial compounds built to not break down, which is exactly what makes them useful and

109
00:07:45,659 --> 00:07:48,723
exactly what makes them a problem. They do not degrade.

110
00:07:48,723 --> 00:07:51,620
They move up food chains and concentrate as they go.

111
00:07:52,870 --> 00:07:58,058
Here we identify, they wrote, extraordinary levels of persistent organic pollutants in the

112
00:07:58,058 --> 00:08:03,130
endemic amphipod fauna from two of the deepest ocean trenches, over ten thousand metres.

113
00:08:04,790 --> 00:08:09,625
The two trenches were the Mariana and the Kermadec. Both over ten kilometres deep.

114
00:08:09,625 --> 00:08:12,750
Both thousands of kilometres from any heavy industry.

115
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:15,478
And the sentence that matters.

116
00:08:15,478 --> 00:08:19,937
Contaminant levels were considerably higher than documented for nearby regions of heavy

117
00:08:19,937 --> 00:08:20,860
industrialization.

118
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:28,196
Higher in the trench than in the industrialised regions nearby. That is the finding.

119
00:08:28,196 --> 00:08:29,750
Not comparable. Higher.

120
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:35,403
I am going to be disciplined here, because this is where this story usually goes wrong.

121
00:08:35,403 --> 00:08:39,457
You will see a specific multiplier quoted for this study all over the internet.

122
00:08:39,457 --> 00:08:42,740
A number of times more contaminated than a named polluted river.

123
00:08:43,970 --> 00:08:48,889
That figure is not in the paper's abstract, the full text sits behind a paywall, and the press

124
00:08:48,889 --> 00:08:52,186
release that carried it is gone. So we are not going to use it.

125
00:08:52,186 --> 00:08:56,844
What the authors published in the open is the sentence you just read, and it is more than

126
00:08:56,844 --> 00:08:57,210
enough.

127
00:08:58,390 --> 00:09:03,079
Their conclusion was that this indicates bioaccumulation of anthropogenic contamination, and

128
00:09:03,079 --> 00:09:07,870
infers that these pollutants are pervasive across the world's oceans, and to full ocean depth.

129
00:09:09,450 --> 00:09:11,920
The third measurement is not in the animals.

130
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:15,400
It is in the mud, and it carries a number I had to read twice.

131
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:20,560
The third measurement is not in the animals at all. It is in the mud they live on.

132
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:26,654
In twenty twenty one, a team took sediment cores from four trench regions, from fifteen hundred

133
00:09:26,654 --> 00:09:31,120
metres down to ten thousand eight hundred and forty, and measured the mercury in them.

134
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:37,690
Mercury is a neurotoxin, and like the organic pollutants, it concentrates as it moves up a food

135
00:09:37,690 --> 00:09:38,030
chain.

136
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:43,717
They found the trenches are accumulating mercury at between twenty four and two hundred and

137
00:09:43,717 --> 00:09:45,902
twenty micrograms per square metre per year.

138
00:09:45,902 --> 00:09:50,270
Greater than the global deep sea average, they wrote, by a factor of up to four hundred.

139
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:53,368
And they traced where it came from.

140
00:09:53,368 --> 00:09:58,220
Most of the mercury in these trenches, they concluded, is derived from the surface ocean.

141
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:04,940
From up here. From the lit, warm, busy part of the ocean that we actually touch.

142
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:08,645
The cores hold the change as a timeline.

143
00:10:08,645 --> 00:10:12,937
Before nineteen hundred, an average of fifty one nanograms per gram.

144
00:10:12,937 --> 00:10:15,020
After nineteen fifty, eighty one.

145
00:10:16,210 --> 00:10:21,092
And the paper is explicit that this rise cannot be explained by a change in how much organic

146
00:10:21,092 --> 00:10:24,170
carbon is falling. Something else had to be delivering it.

147
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:28,893
Which leaves the question the measurements cannot answer on their own.

148
00:10:28,893 --> 00:10:30,760
Why does it end up down there at all?

149
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:32,636
So why there?

150
00:10:32,636 --> 00:10:37,080
Why would the most remote place on Earth hold more of this than the water above it?

151
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:42,770
The mechanism people point to is shape. A trench is a funnel with no outlet.

152
00:10:43,970 --> 00:10:47,942
Everything that sinks in the open ocean drifts down slowly and spreads out.

153
00:10:47,942 --> 00:10:52,709
Everything that sinks near a trench slides down the walls and collects at the bottom, in a

154
00:10:52,709 --> 00:10:54,880
narrow strip, with nowhere further to go.

155
00:10:56,090 --> 00:11:00,145
And at the bottom of that funnel sit the scavengers, eating everything that arrives,

156
00:11:00,145 --> 00:11:03,090
concentrating in their own tissue whatever came down with it.

157
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:09,510
That is the explanation, and it is a good one. But I want to be accurate about its status.

158
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:12,105
It is a mechanism that fits the observations.

159
00:11:12,105 --> 00:11:16,200
It is not a proven cause, and the papers themselves do not claim it is.

160
00:11:17,410 --> 00:11:22,166
There is a lot here that is genuinely unsettled. How fast this is still accumulating.

161
00:11:22,166 --> 00:11:25,524
Whether it is harming the animals or simply sitting in them.

162
00:11:25,524 --> 00:11:30,000
What the fibres do to an amphipod that has evolved for a diet of whatever falls.

163
00:11:31,190 --> 00:11:34,239
And whether it reaches the snailfish that eat the amphipods.

164
00:11:34,239 --> 00:11:36,170
Nobody has published that measurement.

165
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:42,044
Here is what I think this actually is, and it is not the thing these stories usually get turned

166
00:11:42,044 --> 00:11:42,290
into.

167
00:11:43,460 --> 00:11:47,160
The hadal snailfish arrived in the trenches a few million years ago.

168
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:51,131
It gave up its colour, its eyes and the calcium in its bones to be there.

169
00:11:51,131 --> 00:11:54,123
It cruises at seven tenths of a body length per second.

170
00:11:54,123 --> 00:11:57,170
It can smell a meal from three hundred and fifty metres.

171
00:11:58,390 --> 00:12:03,758
And in the same trench, in every single animal a research team pulled up and opened, there was

172
00:12:03,758 --> 00:12:04,500
a blue fibre.

173
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:08,657
Sixty five animals out of ninety, across six trenches.

174
00:12:08,657 --> 00:12:12,406
It is a small sample and it proves less than a headline wants it to.

175
00:12:12,406 --> 00:12:17,589
What it does prove is that there is no longer anywhere on this planet clean enough to use as a

176
00:12:17,589 --> 00:12:18,030
control.

177
00:12:19,230 --> 00:12:24,115
If the deep ocean holds your attention, there is one animal down there nobody has ever seen

178
00:12:24,115 --> 00:12:25,027
alive and intact.

179
00:12:25,027 --> 00:12:29,321
Watch Nobody Has Ever Seen a Full-Grown Colossal Squid, right here on mindwired.

180
00:12:29,321 --> 00:12:33,830
And subscribe to mindwired, so you don't miss the next case we open from the record.

